  "same_product_days_excluded": 3,
  "verdict": "The pre-registered discriminator runs as follows on the retained archive.  (1) Span dependence is confirmed as a dominant systematic: the same estimator on the same product returns lambda from ~1,050 to ~1,935 km across quarterly windows (R2 ranging -0.18 to 0.80), so the 1-year MGEX headline 1,396 km is an unstable estimate of a window-dependent scale, not a product-fixed value.  (2) On strictly matched days with same-product fallbacks excluded by detrended-residual comparison (3 days identified, plus undetermined days conservatively excluded), two independent processing chains with different constellation weightings and software (COD/GPSEST vs GRG/GINS) return pooled lambda = 2744 km (COD, n=43971) and 1933 km (GRG, n=44000) \u2014 the same order and sign, ~30% apart, both above the 1-year headline \u2014 demonstrating the recovered scale is a property of the data, not of one center's processing, and that the 1,396-vs-4,200 km tension is dominated by sampling instability rather than a physical product split.  On all 36 archived dual-center days the COD pool gives 3134 km (n=143938).  (3) A literal per-constellation station-clock split is structurally impossible: MGEX station clocks are single combined-solution parameters (one AR series per station), and the WUM archive carries satellite records only.  The space-segment channel was already excluded by Step 2.8 \u2014 within-constellation satellite-clock coherence shows no distance-decaying structure for any constellation (fits at the 1e5 km boundary or ill-conditioned), so the station-clock scale is not inherited from satellite-clock spatial correlation.  Consequence for Paper 6 issue 6-7: the rho_T anchor should carry an epoch/window systematic of order 30-40% (the observed window scatter), not the 12% inter-center figure, and the Level-1/2/3 hierarchy does not need to absorb a product-level falsification \u2014 the held-out scale is reproducible across independent centers."
}